A||||10||84Bill||WOOOHOO! PA Law baby!- -- -Long||||1||
Z||000000||84Bill||08-31-2001||08:33 PM||||There’s a new law being enforced right now in Pennsylvania that says you can't drive in the left lane. Believe it or not, the point is to keep traffic moving and to keep people safe. But some people are asking if this could actually work. NBC10’s Lu Ann Cahn explains. <BR> <BR>  <BR>PHILADELPHIA - “It give you more safety,” said one driver.<P>“I think it’s a good idea that you use the left side just to pass,” said another driver.<P>Most drivers we spoke to believe the new Pennsylvania law will cut down on road rage, accidents and just the pure frustration of getting stuck behind a pokey driver hogging the left hand lane.<P>The newest version of the law requires drivers to stay to the right unless you are passing, moving over to allow traffic to merge, or you are preparing to make a left hand turn or to exit. State troopers will be handing out tickets to…<P>“Somebody that’s causing traffic from flowing,” said Corporal Robert Whitbeck, from the Pennsylvania State Police. “They’re clogging up the roadway. Somebody coming down the left lane going 10 miles under the speed limit is going to Cause traffic to back up. That’s going be easy to spot.”<P>“I’ve got not problem with it,” said Gary Payne, a truck driver. “I just go down the road, take my time, get my job done, doesn’t matter what lane I’m in.”<P>That’s good because Pennsylvania legislators also clarified the law regarding truckers. Any vehicle over 10,000 pounds must stay right at all times while on roads with three or more lanes.<P>“They move slower,” said Dan Gana, from Port Richmond. “They have less passing power. Keep them on the right. Let the faster moving traffic go by them on that side. Stay out of their blind spots. Causes a lot less accidents.” <P>Those of you caught hogging the left hand lane for more than a couple of miles may find yourselves with a trooper on your tail and a ticket for $25 to $90 with your name on it<BR> <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cheever3000||08-31-2001||08:38 PM||||It'll just be like "where's a cop when you need one?"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||08-31-2001||08:46 PM||||We need a law like that in MD.  I think this will also make it easier for troopers to catch speeders since they can just shoot up the left lane right on someones tail.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-31-2001||09:41 PM||||Most places Ive been thats always been the law. Look it up in your state drivers manual. Thats why its called the 'passing' lane in the first place. I always stay in the right lane except when I pass someone or the left lane is the one you need for a turn or split. Of course it really only applies to non surface streets. In town all lanes are for use. In Europe, you better stay out of the passing lanes or you are going to get rear ended.||||reg||1
