Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||09-01-2001||04:49 AM||||I don't get it. I was under the impression that in the States it was perfectly acceptable (although not legal) to pass left <B>and</B> right on the highway. You know, the "stay in your lane" principle. In fact, when I lived in TX people were always passing left and right and nobody cared. I also noticed that in PA.||||reg||5
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-01-2001||07:24 AM||||Technicly your not supposed to pass on the right here cliff, but thats more for like those people who pass you on the berm or in a no traffic lane. If slower traffic DID stay in the right lane and only used the left lanes to pass, youd never have anyone passing on the right theoreticly. Do you have hiways there like the autobahn where the fast lane is usually moving over 100 mph?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||09-01-2001||09:52 AM||||Roger,<BR>The hand book in Indiana says it is permissable to pass on the right when the way is clear.<BR>Seems to me like that kind of law would be a good on but not very enforceable in states where the big trucks are limited to a 55mph speed limit (Ohio included). Unfortunatly, the majority of interstate highways where this would be a big plus are only two and 3 lanes aside, therefore impratical. Other places have the left lane reserved at posted time for carpoolers (2 or more occupants) during rush hours which turn things into a mess. <BR>Safety issues? Keep everyone out of the left lane for passing only? This just jambs everyone into the other lanes only packed tighter together, gives more safety says one? Sure doesn't sound like it!<BR>Posting a minumium speed for the far left sounds better, like the speed limit period? I'm sure that those of us who use it are doing the speed limit, at the very least. More common would be 5-10 or more above!<BR>The laws that should be entered that may work the best (also un-enforceable) would require to be in the right lane (or left in certain cases) at least 1 mile prior to your exit, as opposed to runing at breakneck speed till the last 100 yards and cuttting over 3 or more lanes of traffic to catch the exit all for a matter of seconds time! We all know that these are the manuevers that causes all sorts of things to happen which happen to cascade into larger and worse problems in the following traffic.<BR>Here is a problem with keeping big trucks in the right lane with three or more lanes. Picture if you will morning rush hour as it is now. Notice all the big trucks scattered in the right two lanes or even in the third one. Now visualize them all in the right lane and you want to get on the road. You get pissed off cause they won't let you into traffic. Why? Well all the trucks are now in the right lane, right? That puts all the cars in the left lanes leaving no place for the big trucks to move to to let you merge.<BR>Why not just designate lanes for thru traffic and local traffic in conjested areas and let it go at that?<P>OOPS! Too long, so I'm a blow hard!||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||09-01-2001||12:01 PM||||For the most part passing on the right is quite common in PA and in most other states as well. I generaly don't like to do that since slower traffic leaving and entering the highway and I just don't feel comfortable doing that. If I'm tooling along at 70mph in the left lane and some one blasts up from behind me I'll move over asap. I dont like it when I'm stuck behind a "slowpoke" even if they are doing 70 and I want to do 75. I think this law will do 2 things\<BR>1 Reduce roadrage by allowing people to move at their own pace.<BR>2 Enable police to clock speeders much easier.<P>Now heavy traffic is a different story but it is just normal to poke along no matter the lane, thats acceptable.<P>I realize I will probably be nailed for speeding with this new law enforced but thats the way it goes. <P>The way I see this law is it just enforces common courtsey. I just hope it works and the coppers do their job<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||09-01-2001||12:34 PM||||Truckers move slower, have less passing power? NOT on I-10 between San Antone and El Paso. Not unusual to see em running 85+. Also, that law is in effect here at least locally. Seems pretty useless since here in West Texas, we have wide smooth shoulders, and slower traffic almost always moves over to the shoulder to let others go around. I've heard that some states outlaw driving on the shoulder. True?||||reg||1
